<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Francigena</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via Francigena was the main medieval road and pilgrim route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, described in the itinerary of Archbishop Sigeric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> around 990. In Tuscany it passed through Lucca, San Gimignano and Siena, whose trade, inns and banking profited from the passage of pilgrims, merchants and armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-francigena.jpg" alt="Via Francigena" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paulusburg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VF_Ruta_completa_con_principales_poblaciones.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
